 <dedication>
  <para>
   This book would have been possible without Lucky, but she was still a
   good cat.
  </para>
 </dedication>

 <preface><title>Introduction</title>
  <para>
   Seminole is a small, multi-threaded web server designed for use in
   embedded systems, and distributed under an extremely liberal
   licensing policy.  It is written in C++ with an eye towards
   portability as well as modularity. The services of the underlying
   operating system are abstracted with a few simple functions and
   <literal>typedef</literal>s.
  </para>

  <para>
   Seminole is designed with two main objectives in mind (according to
   the original author):
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Smallest possible executable size (binary code must be smaller than
     24K on a typical <acronym>MCU</acronym>).
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Good performance on slow <hardware>processors</hardware>.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   This manual is intended for anyone who will be including Seminole in
   another system, using it as a standalone <acronym>HTTP</acronym>
   server, or extending Seminole in some way.  The first chapter
   provides a general overview of Seminole's architecture and runtime
   flow.  A comprehensive class reference follows, with one class per
   chapter, documenting each public method and any other relevant data
   structures.  Finally, one chapter each is devoted to porting
   Seminole to new platforms and writing extensions to Seminole.
  </para>

  <para>
   Comments and corrections concerning this documentation are welcome,
   and may be sent via Internet mail to
   <email>shttpd-general@conman.org</email>.
  </para>

 </preface>

