 <appendix><title>Legalese</title> 
  <para>
   Seminole's license was inspired by, and to some degree copied from,
   the <application>MySQL</application> license.
  </para>

  <para>
   Seminole is distributed under slightly modified terms of the <ulink
   url="http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html"><acronym>GNU</acronym>
   General Public License (<acronym>GPL</acronym>)</ulink>.
  </para>

  <para>
   In general, a commercial Seminole license is required under the
   following circumstances:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     You link a non-free program with code from Seminole.  For
     example, you utilize Seminole as a control mechanism in your
     embedded system product running <productname
     class=registered>VxWorks</productname>, or add non-free
     extensions to Seminole.  Under the standard provisions of the
     <acronym>GPL</acronym>, these examples would be violations unless
     all the components became free software.  Purchasing a commercial
     license means that this will not happen.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     You are distributing a commercial product or application which
     only functions properly with Seminole, and Seminole is shipped
     with the product or application.  This is viewed as a less
     sophisticated form of linking, and hence requires a commercial
     license.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     You are distributing Seminole without providing source code.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Even if your usage of Seminole is not covered by the above
   categories, you can still contribute to the project by purchasing a
   commercial license or support services.
  </para> 

  <para>
   If you're not sure whether a given scenario requires a commercial
   license, we encourage you to contact us via email to make a
   determination.  Such questions can be mailed to
   <email>shttpd-general@conman.org</email>.
  </para>
  
 </appendix> 

 <appendix><title>Getting Help</title>
  <para>
   First, before looking for help elsewhere, please re-read the
   provided documentation (probably the <citetitle
   pubwork="book">Programmer's Manual</citetitle> you're reading now),
   and make sure your question isn't answered already.  We make a
   fairly significant effort to produce high quality, comprehensive
   documentation, but you can't benefit by that unless you read it.
   In the same vein, if you discover documentation errors or find that
   anything that is unclear, please let the project know and it will
   be fixed.
  </para>

  <para>
   If the documentation still doesn't help, it's worth repeating the 
   old adage that the ultimate documentation is the source code
   itself.  Seminole's code is generally well commented and easy to
   follow, and its small size allows complete comprehension.
  </para>

  <para>
   Finally, the Seminole project team operates a World Wide
   Web site at <ulink url="http://www.conman.org/software/seminole">
   http://www.conman.org/software/seminole/</ulink>,
   which contains frequently-asked questions as well as pointers to
   other informational resources.  A bug report submission mechanism
   is also available.
  </para>

  <para>
   The project's resources are very limited, but we will also answer
   questions mailed to <email>shttpd-dev@conman.org</email> on a
   time-available basis.  Please send specific questions with all
   the information appropriate for your situation; if we cannot make
   sense of your problem at all because of insufficient data, we
   unfortunately may have to ignore it.
  </para>   

  <para>
   If you need a more consistent level of support, commercial
   arrangements or consulting services may be a possibility.  Please
   see the project's web site for further information, or send email
   to <email>shttpd-general@conman.org</email>.  
  </para>

 </appendix>

 <colophon>
  <para>
   This manual was produced using <ulink 
   url="http://www.fsf.org/software/emacs/emacs.html">Emacs</ulink> in 
   <literal>psgml</literal> mode.  Processing was performed
   using <ulink
   url="http://openjade.sourceforge.net/">OpenJade</ulink> and
   <ulink url="http://www.netfolder.com/DSSSL/index.html">
   <acronym>DSSSL</acronym></ulink> 
   <ulink
   url="http://www.oasis-open.org/committees/docbook/">DocBook</ulink>
   stylesheets. Final processing for the on-line version was done with
   scripts written in <ulink url="http://www.cpan.org/">Perl</ulink>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Seminole itself was developed and tested on a variety of operating
   systems, including <ulink
   url="http://www.openbsd.org/">OpenBSD</ulink> and <ulink
   url="http://www.linux.org/">Linux</ulink>.  The chief C++ compiler
   used was <ulink
   url="http://www.fsf.org/software/gcc/gcc.html">GCC</ulink>.  And of
   course, the primary ingredient of any software project: 
   <ulink url="http://www.heineken.com">alcohol</ulink>.
  </para>

 </colophon>

</book>
